1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,190
Let's find the position of 7 among these three numbers.

2
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:07,830
On the next line, the list follows the 7.

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:12,200
The word in tells us where to look.

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:18,090
Early Swift required a backtick on each side of that word.

5
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:22,250
In a loop, in is part of Swift’s grammar.

6
00:00:22,870 --> 00:00:26,060
In the call below, it has a different role.

7
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:33,070
Before the colon, it names the value that follows. This name is an argument label.

8
00:00:33,690 --> 00:00:39,640
In November 2015, Doug Gregor proposed allowing keywords in that position.

9
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:45,060
When designing a new function, you could choose within instead.

10
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,320
Importing existing Objective-C code into Swift was a different matter.

11
00:00:50,940 --> 00:00:56,930
This call asks which touches occurred within a view. Here, in fits naturally.

12
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:03,980
The proposal identified nearly 200 imported APIs that needed renaming or backticks.

13
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:09,980
About 90% of the keyword conflicts involved in.

14
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:15,050
The proposal also considered making an exception just for in.

15
00:01:15,670 --> 00:01:20,850
Other keywords, like protocol below, would still need backticks under that approach.

16
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:30,490
The adopted rule uses position. Inside a call, a name followed by a colon can be read as a label.

17
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:35,280
A declaration could now use in before the list as well.

18
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:41,880
In declarations of that era, inout, var and let specified properties of a parameter.

19
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:47,150
Using inout itself as a name still required backticks.

20
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:55,940
Implementation was recorded in December 2015. Swift 2.2 shipped in March 2016.

21
00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:59,770
Let's remove both backticks from the original call.

22
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:07,500
Both calls produce 1 in modern Swift. Array indices start at zero.

23
00:02:08,120 --> 00:02:14,570
The search is unchanged. We can now read in directly as the place to look.
